<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Patrae</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Patrae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Achaean city at the mouth of the Gulf of Corinth, a founder of the Achaean League and forerunner of modern Patras.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Patrae was formed, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-geographer">Pausanias</a>, by uniting the villages of Aroe, Antheia and Mesatis on the coast of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. It was one of the twelve Achaean cities and controlled the narrows at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Patrae was a modest farming and trading town in the Classical period. In 280 BCE it joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dyme">Dyme</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tritaea">Tritaea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharae-achaea">Pharae</a> in reviving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a>. In 279 BCE the Patraeans were the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a> to send troops against the invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gauls</a>, suffering heavy losses. The city had a sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> Laphria.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Patrae passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE. Augustus made it a Roman colony c. 14 BCE, and it became the chief port of the region, growing into the modern city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barony-of-patras">Patras</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/patrae.jpg" alt="Patrae" width="320" height="529"><figcaption>Image: SilentResident (compilation, sources to attribute see above; if any of the sources used in the Montage are removed fr... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patras_Montage_L.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)